.TH "ELEMENT.ATTACHSHADOW" "3JS" "July 9, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.attachShadow \- Element: attachShadow() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]attachShadow()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Element\f[R]
interface attaches a shadow DOM tree to the specified element and
returns a reference to its \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
attachShadow(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R]
An object which contains the following fields:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R]
A string specifying the \f[I]encapsulation mode\f[R] for the shadow DOM
tree.
This can be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]open\f[R]
Elements inside the shadow root are accessible from JavaScript via the
element\(cqs \f[CR]shadowRoot\f[R] property.
.TP
\f[B]closed\f[R]
Elements inside the shadow root cannot be accessed from JavaScript via
the \f[CR]shadowRoot\f[R] property, which is set to \f[CR]null\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]clonable\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A boolean that specifies whether the shadow root is clonable: when set
to \f[CR]true\f[R], the shadow host cloned with
\f[CR]Node.cloneNode()\f[R] or \f[CR]Document.importNode()\f[R] will
include shadow root in the copy.
Its default value is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]customElementRegistry\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A \f[CR]CustomElementRegistry\f[R] that will be used as the scoped
custom element registry of the attached shadow root.
If \f[CR]null\f[R] or \f[CR]undefined\f[R], the shadow root will use the
global registry referenced by \f[CR]Window.customElements\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]delegatesFocus\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A boolean that, when set to \f[CR]true\f[R], specifies behavior that
mitigates custom element issues around focusability.
When a non\-focusable part of the shadow DOM is clicked, the first
focusable part is given focus, and the shadow host is given any
available \f[CR]:focus\f[R] styling.
Its default value is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]referenceTarget\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
A string value that indicates the effective target of any element
reference made against the shadow host from outside the host element.
The value should be the ID of an element inside the shadow DOM.
If set, target references to the host element from outside the shadow
DOM will cause the referenced target element to become the effective
target of the reference to the host element.
.TP
\f[B]serializable\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A boolean that, when set to \f[CR]true\f[R], indicates that the shadow
root is serializable.
If set, the shadow root may be serialized by calling the
\f[CR]Element.getHTML()\f[R] or \f[CR]ShadowRoot.getHTML()\f[R] methods
with the \f[CR]options.serializableShadowRoots\f[R] parameter set
\f[CR]true\f[R].
Its default value is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]slotAssignment\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A string specifying the \f[I]slot assignment mode\f[R] for the shadow
DOM tree.
This can be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]named\f[R]
Elements are automatically assigned to \f[CR]<slot>\f[R] elements within
this shadow root.
Any top\-level children of the host with a \f[CR]slot\f[R] attribute
which matches the \f[CR]name\f[R] attribute of a \f[CR]<slot>\f[R]
within this shadow root will be assigned to that slot.
Any top\-level children of the host with no \f[CR]slot\f[R] attribute
will be assigned to the first \f[CR]<slot>\f[R] with no \f[CR]name\f[R]
attribute (the \(lqdefault slot\(rq), if one is present.
This is the default value.
.TP
\f[B]manual\f[R]
Elements are manually assigned to particular slot elements using
\f[CR]HTMLSlotElement.assign()\f[R].
No automatic assignment takes place.
.RE
.RE
.SS Return value
Returns a \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R] object.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]NotSupportedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
This error may be thrown when you try to attach a shadow root to an
element:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
outside the HTML namespace or that can\(cqt have a shadow attached to
it.
.IP \(bu 2
where the element definition static property \f[CR]disabledFeatures\f[R]
has been given a value of \f[CR]\(dqshadow\(dq\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
that already has a shadow root that was not created declaratively.
.IP \(bu 2
that has a declarative shadow root but the specified \f[CR]mode\f[R]
does not match the existing mode.
.IP \(bu 2
while passing a \f[CR]customElementRegistry\f[R] value that isn\(cqt
\f[CR]null\f[R] or a locally scoped registry (that you created using
\f[CR]new CustomElementRegistry()\f[R]).
The error would be thrown if you passed the global registry.
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]\f[CB]Element.attachShadow()\f[B]\f[R] method attaches a shadow
DOM tree to the specified element and returns a reference to its
\f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R].
.PP
This is the programmatic mechanism to create a \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R],
which is the root node of a Shadow DOM attached to a host element (it is
also possible to create a \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R] declaratively using the
\f[CR]shadowrootmode\f[R] attribute of the \f[CR]<template>\f[R]
element).
It is used for creating custom elements.
.SS Elements you can attach a shadow to
Note that you can\(cqt attach a shadow root to every type of element.
There are some that can\(cqt have a shadow DOM for security reasons (for
example \f[CR]<a>\f[R]).
.PP
The following is a list of elements you \f[I]can\f[R] attach a shadow
root to:
.IP \(bu 2
Any autonomous custom element with a \c
.UR https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#valid-custom-element-name
valid name
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<article>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<aside>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<blockquote>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<body>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<div>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<footer>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h1\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h2\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h3\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h4\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h5\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h6\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<header>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<main>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<nav>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<p>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<section>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<span>\f[R]
.SS Calling this method on an element that is already a shadow host
The method may be called on an element that already has a declarative
shadow root, provided the specified mode \f[CR]mode\f[R] matches the
existing mode.
In this case the \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R] that was already present will be
cleared and returned.
This allows for cases where, for example, server\-side rendering has
already declaratively created a shadow root, and then client\-side code
attempts to attach the root again.
.PP
Otherwise calling \f[CR]attachShadow()\f[R] on an element that already
has a shadow root will throw an exception.
.SS Open and closed shadow roots
A shadow root can be attached with an encapsulation mode, which is
specified as either \f[CR]open\f[R] or \f[CR]closed\f[R].
.PP
If the \f[CR]{mode: \(dqopen\(dq}\f[R] argument is passed, the host
element\(cqs \f[CR]shadowRoot\f[R] property can subsequently be used to
get the attached shadow root.
This can be used to access elements in the Shadow DOM:
.IP
.EX
element.attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq });
element.shadowRoot; \f[I]// Returns a ShadowRoot obj\f[R]
.EE
.PP
If \f[CR]{mode: \(dqclosed\(dq}\f[R] is passed then the Element\(cqs
\f[CR]shadowRoot\f[R] property is set to \f[CR]null\f[R].
Note that JavaScript can still access a closed shadow root by storing
the value returned by the function.
.IP
.EX
element.attachShadow({ mode: \(dqclosed\(dq });
element.shadowRoot; \f[I]// Returns null\f[R]
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Word count custom element
The following example is taken from our \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/web-components-examples/tree/main/word-count-web-component
word\-count\-web\-component
.UE \c
\ demo (\c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/web-components-examples/word-count-web-component/
see it live also
.UE \c
).
You can see that we use \f[CR]attachShadow()\f[R] in the middle of the
code to create a shadow root, which we then attach our custom
element\(cqs contents to.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Create a class for the element\f[R]
\f[B]class\f[R] WordCount \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLParagraphElement {
  constructor() {
    \f[I]// Always call super first in constructor\f[R]
    \f[B]super\f[R]();

    \f[I]// count words in element\(aqs parent element\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] wcParent = \f[B]this\f[R].parentNode;

    \f[B]function\f[R] countWords(node) {
      \f[B]const\f[R] text = node.innerText || node.textContent;
      \f[B]return\f[R] text
        .trim()
        .split(/\(rss+/g)
        .filter((a) \f[B]=>\f[R] a.trim().length > 0).length;
    }

    \f[B]const\f[R] count = \(gaWords: ${countWords(wcParent)}\(ga;

    \f[I]// Create a shadow root\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] shadow = \f[B]this\f[R].attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq });

    \f[I]// Create text node and add word count to it\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] text = document.createElement(\(dqspan\(dq);
    text.textContent = count;

    \f[I]// Append it to the shadow root\f[R]
    shadow.appendChild(text);

    \f[I]// Update count when element content changes\f[R]
    \f[B]this\f[R].parentNode.addEventListener(\(dqinput\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
      text.textContent = \(gaWords: ${countWords(wcParent)}\(ga;
    });
  }
}

\f[I]// Define the new element\f[R]
customElements.define(\(dqword\-count\(dq, WordCount, { extends: \(dqp\(dq });
.EE
.SS Disabling shadow DOM
If the element has a static property named \f[CR]disabledFeatures\f[R],
which is an array containing the string \f[CR]\(dqshadow\(dq\f[R], then
the \f[CR]attachShadow()\f[R] call will throw an exception.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] MyCustomElement \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
  \f[I]// Disable shadow DOM for this element.\f[R]
  \f[B]static\f[R] disabledFeatures = [\(dqshadow\(dq];

  constructor() {
    \f[B]super\f[R]();
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    \f[I]// Create a shadow root.\f[R]
    \f[I]// This will throw an exception.\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] shadow = \f[B]this\f[R].attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq });
  }
}

\f[I]// Define the new element\f[R]
customElements.define(\(dqmy\-custom\-element\(dq, MyCustomElement);
.EE
.SS Named slot assignment
This example demonstrates named slot assignment.
.SS Creating the web component
This code creates a web component that has three named slots for an
article\(cqs title, metadata, and body section.
.PP
The \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R] is attached in the custom element\(cqs
constructor.
We don\(cqt need to explicitly set the option
\f[CR]slotAssignment: \(dqnamed\(dq\f[R] because it is the default.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] MyArticle \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    \f[B]super\f[R]();
    \f[I]// Attach the shadow root\f[R]
    \f[B]this\f[R].attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq \f[I]/* , slotAssignment: \(dqnamed\(dq */\f[R] });
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    \f[B]this\f[R].render();
  }

  render() {
    \f[I]// Define the internal structure and styles\f[R]
    \f[B]this\f[R].shadowRoot.innerHTML = \(ga
      <style>
        .header {
          background\-color: plum;
        }
        .meta {
          background\-color: green;
        }
        .body {
          background\-color: lightblue;
        }
      </style>

      <h2 class=\(dqheader\(dq>
        <slot name=\(dqtitle\(dq></slot>
      </h2>

      <div class=\(dqmeta\(dq>
        <slot name=\(dqmeta\(dq></slot>
      </div>

      <div class=\(dqbody\(dq>
        <slot></slot>
      </div>
    \(ga;
  }
}

\f[I]// Register the component\f[R]
customElements.define(\(dqmy\-article\(dq, MyArticle);
.EE
.SS Using the web component
The HTML below uses the \f[CR]<my\-article>\f[R] web component we just
created.
The nested elements are rendered in the component\(cqs slots based on
name matching.
The unnamed elements are rendered in the component\(cqs unnamed slot
(the body).
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]my\-article\f[R]>
  <\f[B]span\f[R] slot=\(dqtitle\(dq>Text for the title slot</\f[B]span\f[R]>
  <\f[B]span\f[R] slot=\(dqmeta\(dq>Text for the meta slot</\f[B]span\f[R]>

  <\f[B]p\f[R]>
    Text 1 with no slot attribute. Goes into default (unnamed) slot inside the
    \(dqbody\(dq div.
  </\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>
    Text 2 with no slot attribute. Also goes into default (unnamed) slot inside
    the \(dqbody\(dq div.
  </\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]my\-article\f[R]>
.EE
.SS Results
The example below should show the content of the slots displayed in the
appropriate sections.
.SS Unnamed slot assignment
This example demonstrates manual slot assignment.
With this approach, each element must be manually assigned to a
particular slot using \f[CR]HTMLSlotElement.assign()\f[R].
There is no default assignment, so any slot that is not assigned will be
empty.
.SS HTML
First we have a hidden support warning, displayed via JavaScript if the
browser doesn\(cqt support \f[CR]slotAssignment: \(dqmanual\(dq\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqsupport\-warning\(dq hidden>
  ⛔ Your browser doesn\(aqt support manual slot assignment (named assignment is
  used).
</\f[B]p\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
Next, we define our \f[CR]<my\-article>\f[R] custom element with child
elements for the title, metadata, and body content.
Each child is identified by \f[CR]id\f[R]; unlike named slot assignment,
no \f[CR]slot\f[R] attribute is needed.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]my\-article\f[R]>
  <\f[B]span\f[R] id=\(dqtext_title\(dq>Text for the title slot</\f[B]span\f[R]>
  <\f[B]span\f[R] id=\(dqtext_meta\(dq>Text for the meta slot</\f[B]span\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqtext_body_1\(dq>Text 1 for body slot.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqtext_body_2\(dq>Text 2 for body slot.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]my\-article\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The custom element attaches a shadow root with
\f[CR]slotAssignment: \(dqmanual\(dq\f[R].
The shadow DOM contains unnamed slots identified by \f[CR]id\f[R].
The \f[CR]assignSlots()\f[R] method manually assigns the light DOM
elements to the slots.
Note that multiple nodes can be assigned to a single slot \(em the order
they are specified controls the render order.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] MyArticle \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    \f[B]super\f[R]();
    \f[B]this\f[R].attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq, slotAssignment: \(dqmanual\(dq });
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    \f[B]this\f[R].render();
    \f[B]this\f[R].assignSlots();
  }

  render() {
    \f[B]this\f[R].shadowRoot.innerHTML = \(ga
      <style>
        .header {
          background\-color: plum;
        }
        .meta {
          background\-color: green;
        }
        .body {
          background\-color: lightblue;
        }
      </style>

      <h2 class=\(dqheader\(dq>
        <slot id=\(dqtitleSlot\(dq></slot>
      </h2>

      <div class=\(dqmeta\(dq>
        <slot id=\(dqmetaSlot\(dq></slot>
      </div>

      <div class=\(dqbody\(dq>
        <slot id=\(dqbodySlot\(dq></slot>
      </div>
    \(ga;
  }

  assignSlots() {
    \f[I]// 1. Target your slots\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] titleSlot = \f[B]this\f[R].shadowRoot.querySelector(\(dq#titleSlot\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] metaSlot = \f[B]this\f[R].shadowRoot.querySelector(\(dq#metaSlot\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] bodySlot = \f[B]this\f[R].shadowRoot.querySelector(\(dq#bodySlot\(dq);

    \f[I]// 2. Target your light DOM elements\f[R]
    \f[B]const\f[R] titleText = \f[B]this\f[R].querySelector(\(dq#text_title\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] metaText = \f[B]this\f[R].querySelector(\(dq#text_meta\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] body1Text = \f[B]this\f[R].querySelector(\(dq#text_body_1\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] body2Text = \f[B]this\f[R].querySelector(\(dq#text_body_2\(dq);

    \f[I]// 3. Manually assign them\f[R]
    titleSlot.assign(titleText);
    metaSlot.assign(metaText);
    bodySlot.assign(body2Text, body1Text);
  }
}

customElements.define(\(dqmy\-article\(dq, MyArticle);
.EE
.PP
This code tests if the \f[CR]ShadowRoot.slotAssignment\f[R] property is
defined, and displays the warning if it is not.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] isSlotAssignmentSupported = Object.hasOwn(
  ShadowRoot.prototype,
  \(dqslotAssignment\(dq,
);

document
  .querySelector(\(dqp[hidden]\(dq)
  .toggleAttribute(\(dqhidden\(dq, isSlotAssignmentSupported);
.EE
.SS Results
The example below should show the content of the slots displayed in the
appropriate sections.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If manual slot assignment is not supported, a warning is
displayed and the browser will use \f[CR]named\f[R] assignment.
However, because none of the light DOM elements have a \f[CR]slot\f[R]
attribute, they will all be inserted into the first unnamed slot (the
title slot).
.RE
